/// As we discussed earlier; objects have an effect called "late binding".
///
/// This means that you can kind of think of classes and objects as functions.
/// For example, here is a really contrived class that represents the addition function.
///
/// The `fixed` keyword means: "this property cannot be amended or assigned to".
/// It's there to telegraph to the user of this API that "this is an output property only".
hidden __section_1__ {}

class Addition {
  /// The first number to be added
  a: Number

  /// The second number to be added
  b: Number

  /** Fixed means: keep adding */
  /// The result
  fixed result: Number = a + b
}

function add(a: Number, b: Number) = a + b

/// The [Addition] class is basically the same as the [add] function, but the way they're called is different.
///
/// Using an object to represent this computation means that you can "partially apply" a function.
/// You can create an object that only has some of the inputs defined, and fill them out later via amending.
///
/// You can also define multiple results by adding more properties that are computed in terms of other properties.
hidden __section_2__ {}

res1 = add(1, 2)

local base: Addition = new { a = 1 }

res2 = (base) { b = 2 }.result
